\begin_body

\begin_layout Section
Setting
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The market and the fund investors have fundamentally different abilities to learn from a manager's trading history.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Manager's Behavior and Financial Market
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A continuum of managers,
 indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in I$
\end_inset

,
 make investments in a two-period economy (
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,2$
\end_inset

).
 At the beginning of 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

,
 manager 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 draws her skill 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma_{s}^{2})$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent across 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in I$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
In 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

,
 manager 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 identifies a single risky asset (asset 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{i}$
\end_inset

) with payoff 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{A_{i}}=\bar{\delta}+s_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 She trades this asset in both periods by placing a market order for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A_{i},t}$
\end_inset

 units in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 The payoff 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{A_{i}}$
\end_inset

 is realized in the end of 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2$
\end_inset

,
 manager 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 identifies another asset (asset 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{i}$
\end_inset

) whose payoff is 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{B_{i}}=\bar{\delta}+s_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 She trades this asset only in 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2$
\end_inset

 by placing a market order for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{B_{i},2}$
\end_inset

 units,
 and the payoff 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{B_{i}}$
\end_inset

 is realized in the end of 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We may extend this setting to OLG so that both assets are long-lived.
 But the essential point here is that two assets have different tracking record from market makers' perspective,
 as specified below.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
For each asset,
 there is a competitive market maker and a noise trader.
 The noise trader places a random market order for 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{k,t}$
\end_inset

 units of asset 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{k,t}\sim N(0,\sigma_{u}^{2})$
\end_inset

 is iid across 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 The market maker for a particular asset observes the total order flow and sets the price 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k,t}$
\end_inset

.
 Importantly,
 the financial market is anonymous.
 Together with the fact that there is a continuum of managers trading a continuum of assets,
 the anonymity implies the following:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
For assets 
\begin_inset Formula $\{B_{i}\}_{i\in I}$
\end_inset

,
 market makers do not use information in 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

 to update her belief about 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{B_{i}}$
\end_inset

.
 Since a manager picks different assets in 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2$
\end_inset

,
 even though the market maker knows there is informed trading by a manager,
 she cannot infer which asset this informed manager has traded in 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For assets 
\begin_inset Formula $\{A_{i}\}_{i\in I}$
\end_inset

,
 market makers use information in 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

 to update their belief about 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{A_{i}}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2$
\end_inset

.
 This is because a single asset is traded twice only if the same manager trades it across 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2$
\end_inset

,
 thereby allowing the market maker to use 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{A_{i},1}=x_{A_{i},1}+u_{A_{i},1}$
\end_inset

 to update her belief.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection
Investor's Behavior
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two alternative settings of investor capital allocation and the fund structure,
 while setting 2 is not feasible because a symmetric Nash equilibrium cannot be obtained.
 In both setting,
 consider investor 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 allocating capital 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{j,i,t}$
\end_inset

 to manager 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

,
 while the total capital flow to manager 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{i,t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
(Separate vintage;
 closed-end fund).
 The manager holds fund 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{i,1}$
\end_inset

 in the first period,
 which is specified by her position in asset 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{i}$
\end_inset

 (i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A_{i},1}$
\end_inset

) and the risk-free asset.
 In 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2$
\end_inset

,
 she forms another fund 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{i,2}$
\end_inset

 that consists of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A,2}$
\end_inset

 units of asset 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{B,2}$
\end_inset

 units of asset 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 (and the risk-free asset).
 Each fund generates fund proceeds 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{i,1}=(\delta_{A_{i},1}-p_{A_{i},1})x_{A_{i},1}+M_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{i,2}=\sum_{k=A,B}(\delta_{k_{i},1}-p_{k_{i},1})x_{k_{i},1}+M_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 After taking 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 fraction of 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 as a fee,
 the manager allocates 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 to her investors according to their contributions.
 Hence,
 investor 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 receives 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{j}=\sum_{t=1,2}(1-\phi)\frac{m_{j,i,t}}{M_{i,t}}V_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 in the end of 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
(NAV-based payoff;
 open-end fund).
 By denoting the fund's total proceeds as 
\begin_inset Formula $V=\sum_{t=1,2}(\delta_{A_{i},t}-p_{A_{i},t})x_{A_{i},t}+(\delta_{B_{i},2}-p_{B_{i},2})x_{B_{i},2}+M_{1}+M_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 the investor's end-of-period payoff is proportional to her total share:
 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{j}=\frac{\sum_{t=1,2}m_{j,i,t}}{\sum_{t=1,2}M_{i,t}}(1-\phi)V$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the share is computed based on NAV.
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In this case,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
I=\frac{m_{j,1}+m_{j,2}}{M}(1-\phi)\Pi-\phi(m_{j,1}+m_{j,2})
\]

\end_inset

 the FOC is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{M-(m_{j,1}+m_{j,2})}{M^{2}}(1-\phi)\Pi=\phi
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
m_{j,2}=\sqrt{(\sum_{t=1,2}\sum_{l\neq j}m_{l,t})\frac{1-\phi}{\phi}E[\Pi]}-M_{1}-\sum_{l\neq j}m_{l,2}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

 As the share depends on the capital allocations in both periods,
 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{j,i,2}$
\end_inset

 depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{j,i,1}$
\end_inset

.
 Although the symmetric equilibrium in 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2$
\end_inset

 may exist under the symmetric assumption in 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

,
 we cannot rule out profitable deviation in 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following note adopts setting 1.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Another issue is about the population of investors.
 If we do not endogenize the distribution of investors across managers,
 both settings yield the same result.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Assume that there is a continuum of investors in each period,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{t}$
\end_inset

 is determined by the free entry condition period-by-period.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Assume that each manager has 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 investors,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 is exogenously given and fixed throughout the periods.
 Endogenous 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 is not feasible:
 the equilibrium variables depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 through flow-performance sensitivity 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\frac{n-1}{n}\frac{1-\phi}{\phi}$
\end_inset

.
 Deriving 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 by the free-entry condition is complicated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At the end of 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

,
 manager 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 tells her investors about the noisy information about her period-1 investment in asset 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 that is 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A,1}.$
\end_inset

 The signal is 
\begin_inset Formula $f=x_{A,1}+\eta$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta\sim N(0,\sigma_{\eta}^{2})$
\end_inset

 which can be used to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The model is symmetric across managers 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in I$
\end_inset

,
 and we can focus on a generic manager by omitting manager index 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Period 2 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Financial Market
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The manager trades two assets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 We guess and later verify the following linear strategies:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{A,2}=\beta_{A,2}(\delta_{A}-p_{A,1}),\ p_{A,2}=p_{A,1}+\lambda_{A,2}y_{A,2}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{B,2}=\beta_{B,2}s,\ p_{B,2}=\bar{\delta}+\lambda_{B,2}y_{B,2}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Given the price conjecture and noting that 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{2}$
\end_inset

 is already fixed,
 the manager in 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2$
\end_inset

 solves
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{x_{A,2},x_{B,2}}\mathrm{E}\left[\sum_{k=A,B}(\delta_{k}-p_{k,2})x_{k,2}|s\right]=(\delta_{A}-p_{A,1}-\lambda_{A,2}x_{A,2})x_{A,2}+(s-\lambda_{B,2}x_{B,2})x_{B,2}.
\]

\end_inset

The FOCs lead to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A,2}=\frac{\delta_{A}-p_{A,1}}{2\lambda_{A,2}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{B,2}=\frac{s}{2\lambda_{B,2}}$
\end_inset

,
 suggesting that 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{k,2}=\frac{1}{2\lambda_{k,2}}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $k=A,B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The market maker of asset 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 knows that the manager is trading the asset again,
 so she uses 
\begin_inset Formula $\{y_{A,1},y_{A,2}\}$
\end_inset

 to compute the price:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{A,2}=p_{A,1}+\mathrm{E}[(\delta-p_{A,1})|y_{A,1},y_{A,2}]=p_{A,1}+\lambda_{A,2}y_{A,2}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\lambda_{A,2}=\frac{\beta_{A,2}\sigma_{A}^{2}}{\beta_{A,2}^{2}\sigma_{A}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}}
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\sigma_{A}^{2} & \equiv\mathrm{Var}[\delta-p_{A,1}|y_{A,1}]=\mathrm{Var}[s|y_{A,1}]\nonumber \\
 & =\frac{\sigma_{s}^{2}\sigma_{u}^{2}}{\beta_{A,1}^{2}\sigma_{s}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}}.\label{eq:sigmaA}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The market maker of asset 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 does not know which manager is trading this asset.
 Hence,
 she rely only on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{B,2}$
\end_inset

 and computes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{B,2}=\mathrm{E}[\delta|y_{B,2}]=\bar{\delta}+\lambda_{B,2}y_{B,2},
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\lambda_{B,2}=\frac{\beta_{B,2}\sigma_{s}^{2}}{\beta_{B,2}^{2}\sigma_{s}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Combining with 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{k,2}=\frac{1}{2\lambda_{k,2}},$
\end_inset

the equilibrium strategies in 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2$
\end_inset

 are
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\beta_{A,2}=\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{A}},\ \lambda_{A,2}=\frac{\sigma_{A}}{2\sigma_{u}},
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\beta_{B,2}=\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{s}},\ \lambda_{B,2}=\frac{\sigma_{s}}{2\sigma_{u}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The period-2 realized profit of fund 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi_{A,2}=(\delta_{A}-p_{A,2})x_{A,2}=\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{A}}(\delta_{A}-p_{A,1})^{2}-\lambda_{A,2}u_{A,2}x_{A,2}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\therefore\mathrm{E}[\pi_{A,2}|s]=\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{A}}\left[(s-\lambda_{A,1}x_{A,1})^{2}+\lambda_{A,1}^{2}\sigma_{u}^{2}\right]
\]

\end_inset

The period-2 realized profit of fund 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi_{B,2}=\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{s}}s^{2}-\frac{1}{2}u_{B,2}s.
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\therefore\mathrm{E}[\pi_{B,2}|s]=\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{s}}s^{2}.\label{eq:pib2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus,
 the expected fund proceeds from fund 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

,
 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V_{2}=\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{A}}\left[(s-\lambda_{A,1}x_{A,1})^{2}+\lambda_{A,1}^{2}\sigma_{u}^{2}\right]+\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{s}}s^{2}+M_{2}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Investors' Capital Allocation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Investors in 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2$
\end_inset

 allocate capital to the manager based on information 
\begin_inset Formula $F=\{f\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Consider investor 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

.
 Her return is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{m_{j,2}}{M_{2}}(1-\phi)(\sum_{k=A,B}\pi_{k,2}+M_{2})-m_{j,2}=\frac{m_{j,2}}{M_{2}}(1-\phi)\sum_{k=A,B}\pi_{k,2}-\phi m_{j,2}.
\]

\end_inset

 By taking the expectation conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{A,1}=x_{A,1}+u_{A,1}$
\end_inset

 and taking the FOC,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
m_{j,2}=\left(\frac{1-\phi}{\phi}\mathrm{E}\left[\sum_{k=A,B}\pi_{k,2}|f\right]\sum_{l\neq j}m_{l,2}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}-\sum_{l\neq i}m_{l,2}.
\]

\end_inset

At the symmetric equilibrium,
 where all investors of the manager allocate the same amount of capital,
 it reduces to 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
m_{2}=\frac{n-1}{n^{2}}\frac{1-\phi}{\phi}\mathrm{E}\left[\sum_{k=A,B}\pi_{k,2}|f\right],
\]

\end_inset

so that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
M_{2}=\theta\mathrm{E}\left[\sum_{k=A,B}\pi_{k,2}|f\right]\label{eq:MB}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\equiv\frac{n-1}{n}\frac{1-\phi}{\phi}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi_{A,2}=\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{A}}\left(s-\lambda_{A,1}(x_{A,1}+u_{A,1})\right)^{2}-\lambda_{A,2}u_{A,2}x_{A,2}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi_{B,2}=\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{s}}s^{2}-\frac{1}{2}u_{B,2}s,
\]

\end_inset

so that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathrm{E}\left[\sum_{k=A,B}\pi_{k,2}|f\right]=\mathrm{E}\left[\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{s}}s^{2}+\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{A}}\left(s-\lambda_{A,1}(x_{A,1}+u_{A,1})\right)^{2}|f\right].
\]

\end_inset

Expanding further,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathrm{E}\left[\left(s-\lambda_{A,1}(x_{A,1}+u_{A,1})\right)^{2}|f\right]=\mathrm{E}\left[\left(s-\lambda_{A,1}(x_{A,1}+u_{A,1})\right)|f\right]^{2}+\mathrm{Var}\left[\left(s-\lambda_{A,1}(x_{A,1}+u_{A,1})\right)|f\right]
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathrm{E}\left[s-\lambda_{A,1}(x_{A,1}+u_{A,1})|f\right]=(1-\lambda_{A,1}\beta_{A,1})\mathrm{E}\left[s|f\right],
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathrm{Var}\left[\left(s-\lambda_{A,1}(x_{A,1}+u_{A,1})\right)|f\right]=(1-\lambda_{A,1}\beta_{A,1})^{2}\mathrm{Var}\left[s|f\right]+\lambda_{A,1}^{2}\sigma_{u}^{2}.
\]

\end_inset

Since the variance decomposition implies
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathrm{E}[s^{2}|f]=\mathrm{E}[s|f]^{2}+\mathrm{Var}[s|f]
\]

\end_inset

we obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{E}\left[\sum_{k=A,B}\pi_{k,2}|f\right] & =\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{s}}\left(\mathrm{E}[s|f]^{2}+\mathrm{Var}[s|f]\right)\\
 & +\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{A}}\left[(1-\lambda_{A,1}\beta_{A,1})^{2}\mathrm{E}\left[s|f\right]^{2}+(1-\lambda_{A,1}\beta_{A,1})^{2}\mathrm{Var}\left[s|f\right]+\lambda_{A,1}^{2}\sigma_{u}^{2}\right]\\
 & =\frac{\sigma_{u}}{2}\left(\frac{1}{\sigma_{s}}+\frac{(1-\lambda_{A,1}\beta_{A,1})^{2}}{\sigma_{A}}\right)\left(\mathrm{E}\left[s|f\right]^{2}+\mathrm{Var}\left[s|f\right]\right)+\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{A}}\lambda_{A,1}^{2}\sigma_{u}^{2}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The updated skill estimate is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{E}[s|f] & =\mathrm{E}[s|\beta_{A,1}s+\eta]=\gamma(x_{A,1}+\eta)
\end{align*}

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\gamma=\frac{\beta_{A,1}\sigma_{s}^{2}}{\beta_{A,1}^{2}\sigma_{s}^{2}+\sigma_{\eta}^{2}},
\]

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{Var}[s|f]=\frac{\sigma_{\eta}^{2}\sigma_{s}^{2}}{\beta_{A,1}^{2}\sigma_{s}^{2}+\sigma_{\eta}^{2}}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathrm{E}[s^{2}|f]=\mathrm{E}\left[s|f\right]^{2}+\mathrm{Var}\left[s|f\right]=\frac{\sigma_{\eta}^{2}\sigma_{s}^{2}}{\beta_{A,1}^{2}\sigma_{s}^{2}+\sigma_{\eta}^{2}}+\gamma^{2}(x_{A,1}+\eta)^{2}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Period 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Financial Market
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
At the beginning of 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

,
 the manager's expected payoff is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi\mathrm{E}[\sum_{t=1,2}\pi_{A,t}+M_{1}+\pi_{B,2}+M_{2}|s].
\]

\end_inset

At this stage,
 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{1}$
\end_inset

 is already fixed,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{B,2}$
\end_inset

 is independent of her period-1 trading strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A,1}$
\end_inset

 (see [
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:pib2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

]).
 However,
 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{A,2}$
\end_inset

 depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A,1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
By focusing on terms related to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 the manager maximizes the following expected profit in 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{x_{A,1}}(s-\lambda_{A,1}x_{A,1})x_{A,1}+\theta\frac{\sigma_{u}}{2}\left(\frac{1}{\sigma_{s}}+\frac{(1-\lambda_{A,1}\beta_{A,1})^{2}}{\sigma_{A}}\right)\gamma^{2}x_{A,1}^{2}+\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{A}}(s-\lambda_{A,1}x_{A,1})^{2},
\]

\end_inset

where the first term comes from the trading profit in 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

,
 the second term comes from 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 and the last term comes from 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{A,2}$
\end_inset

.
 The FOC is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s-2\lambda_{A,1}x_{A,1}+\theta\sigma_{u}\left(\frac{1}{\sigma_{s}}+\frac{(1-\lambda_{A,1}\beta_{A,1})^{2}}{\sigma_{A}}\right)\gamma^{2}x_{A,1}-\lambda_{A,1}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{A}}(s-\lambda_{A,1}x_{A,1})=0
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\therefore x_{A,1}=\frac{1-\lambda_{A,1}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{A}}}{2\lambda_{A,1}-\theta\sigma_{u}\left(\frac{1}{\sigma_{s}}+\frac{(1-\lambda_{A,1}\beta_{A,1})^{2}}{\sigma_{A}}\right)\gamma^{2}-\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{A}}\lambda_{A,1}^{2}}s.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The market maker's pricing is standard,
 leading to 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{A,1}=\frac{\beta_{A,1}\sigma_{s}^{2}}{\beta_{A,1}^{2}\sigma_{s}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}}.$
\end_inset

 Hence,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
1-\lambda_{A,1}\beta_{A,1}=\frac{\sigma_{u}^{2}}{\beta_{A,1}^{2}\sigma_{s}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}}
\]

\end_inset

This leads to 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{A}^{2}=\sigma_{s}^{2}(1-\beta_{A,1}\lambda_{A,1})=\frac{\sigma_{s}^{2}\sigma_{u}^{2}}{\beta_{A,1}^{2}\sigma_{s}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Denote 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{s}}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $b=\frac{\sigma_{\eta}}{\sigma_{s}}$
\end_inset

.
 Also,
 to focus on mathematical analyses,
 we drop subscripts.
 Then,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=\frac{\beta}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{A}^{2}=\frac{\rho^{2}}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}\sigma_{s}^{2},$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\lambda\beta=\frac{\rho^{2}}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=\frac{\beta}{\beta^{2}+b^{2}}$
\end_inset

.
 The equilibrium trading strategy is determined by 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=F(\beta)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
F & =\frac{1-\frac{\beta}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sqrt{\frac{\rho^{2}}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}\sigma_{s}^{2}}}}{2\frac{\beta}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}-\theta\sigma_{u}\left(\frac{1}{\sigma_{s}}+\frac{\left(\frac{\rho^{2}}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}\right)^{2}}{\sqrt{\frac{\rho^{2}}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}\sigma_{s}^{2}}}\right)\left(\frac{\beta}{\beta^{2}+b^{2}}\right)^{2}-\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sqrt{\frac{\rho^{2}}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}\sigma_{s}^{2}}}\left(\frac{\beta}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}\right)^{2}}\\
 & =\frac{1-\sqrt{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}\frac{\beta}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}}{2\frac{\beta}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}-\theta\rho\left(1+\frac{\sqrt{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}}{\rho}\left(\frac{\rho^{2}}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}\right)^{2}\right)\left(\frac{\beta}{\beta^{2}+b^{2}}\right)^{2}-\sqrt{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}\left(\frac{\beta}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}\right)^{2}}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Denote 
\begin_inset Formula $z=\beta/\rho$
\end_inset

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
z & =F/\rho=\frac{1-\sqrt{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}\frac{\beta}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}}{2\frac{\beta\rho}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}-\theta\rho^{2}\left(1+\frac{\sqrt{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}}{\rho}\left(\frac{\rho^{2}}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}\right)^{2}\right)\left(\frac{\beta}{\beta^{2}+b^{2}}\right)^{2}-\rho\sqrt{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}\left(\frac{\beta}{\beta^{2}+\rho^{2}}\right)^{2}}\\
 & =\frac{1-\sqrt{z^{2}+1}\frac{z}{z^{2}+1}}{2\frac{z}{z^{2}+1}-\theta\left(1+\sqrt{z^{2}+1}\left(\frac{1}{z^{2}+1}\right)^{2}\right)\left(\frac{z}{z^{2}+\kappa^{2}}\right)^{2}-\sqrt{z^{2}+1}\left(\frac{z}{z^{2}+1}\right)^{2}}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa=b/\rho=\sigma_{\eta}/\sigma_{u}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Proof
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fixed point problem is given by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
z & =F(z)\equiv\frac{1-\sqrt{z^{2}+1}\frac{z}{z^{2}+1}}{2\frac{z}{z^{2}+1}-\theta\left(1+\sqrt{z^{2}+1}\left(\frac{1}{z^{2}+1}\right)^{2}\right)\left(\frac{z}{z^{2}+\kappa^{2}}\right)^{2}-\sqrt{z^{2}+1}\left(\frac{z}{z^{2}+1}\right)^{2}}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

 are positive parameters.
 Also,
 
\begin_inset Formula $z>0$
\end_inset

.
 Rearranging it with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

,
 it becomes 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\theta=G(z)\equiv\frac{(z^{2}+\kappa^{2})^{2}\left[z^{4}-1+z\left(z^{2}+1\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}\right]}{z^{3}(z^{2}+1)^{2}\left(1+\left(z^{2}+1\right)^{-\frac{3}{2}}\right)}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Firstly,
 we focus on 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A(z)=z^{4}-1+z\left(z^{2}+1\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}>0.
\]

\end_inset

Otherwise,
 there is no solution.
 
\begin_inset Formula $A(z)$
\end_inset

 is monotonically increasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A(z)=0$
\end_inset

 has a unique solution 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{0}\approx0.66$
\end_inset

.
 Hence,
 there is no solution to the fixed point problem,
 and we focus on 
\begin_inset Formula $z>z_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Secondly,
 consider 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{dG}{dz}\propto g(z)=T(z)-\kappa^{2}S(z),
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
T(z)/z^{2}=z^{6}+5z^{4}+3z^{2}-1+\left[-z^{5}+4z^{4}+z^{3}+z^{2}+2z-1\right]\left(z^{2}+1\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}+2z
\]

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S(z)=3z^{6}-z^{4}-7z^{2}+2z-3+\left[5z^{5}+7z^{3}-z^{2}+2z-3\right]\left(z^{2}+1\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}.
\]

\end_inset

As we know 
\begin_inset Formula $z>z_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $5z^{4}>\left(z^{2}+1\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}z^{5}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $2z>1$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
 all negative terms in 
\begin_inset Formula $T(z)/z^{2}$
\end_inset

 are more than offset by other terms,
 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $T(z)>0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

.
 Moreover,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{dS(z)}{dz} & =2z(9z^{2}+7)(z^{4}-1)+2+\left\{ 20z^{6}+39z^{4}-z^{3}+21z^{2}+z+2\right\} \left(z^{2}+1\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}\\
 & >-2z(9z^{2}+7)z(1+z^{2})^{\frac{1}{2}}+2+\left\{ 20z^{6}+39z^{4}-z^{3}+21z^{2}+z+2\right\} \left(z^{2}+1\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}\\
 & \propto-2z^{2}(9z^{2}+7)(1+z^{2})+2(1+z^{2})^{\frac{1}{2}}+\left\{ 20z^{6}+39z^{4}-z^{3}+21z^{2}+z+2\right\} \\
 & =2z^{6}+7z^{4}-z^{3}+7z^{2}+z+2+2(1+z^{2})^{\frac{1}{2}}\\
 & >0,
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where the first and the last inequalities come from 
\begin_inset Formula $A(z)>0$
\end_inset

 (and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $z>z_{0}>0.6$
\end_inset

).
 Thus,
 
\begin_inset Formula $S(z)$
\end_inset

 is monotonically increasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

.
 Also,
 evaluating at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
S(z_{0}) & =-2z_{0}^{7}-3z_{0}^{5}+z_{0}^{3}(z_{0}-2)+(z_{0}^{2}-3)+z_{0}(z_{0}-1)\\
 & <0,
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where we apply 
\begin_inset Formula $A(z_{0})=0$
\end_inset

,
 and the last inequality is due to 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{0}<1$
\end_inset

.
 It is also easy to check 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{z\rightarrow\infty}S(z)=\infty>0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
 
\begin_inset Formula $S(z)=0$
\end_inset

 has a unique solution in 
\begin_inset Formula $z>z_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 which we denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 we want to characterize the behavior of 
\begin_inset Formula $H(z)\equiv\frac{T(z)}{S(z)}$
\end_inset

.
 Namely,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{0}<z<z_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $S<0$
\end_inset

,
 and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $G'(z)>0$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is monotonically increasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}\le z$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $G'(z)>0$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{T(z)}{S(z)}=H(z)>\kappa^{2}$
\end_inset

,
 while 
\begin_inset Formula $G'(z)<0$
\end_inset

 otherwise.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $S(z)\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $z\rightarrow z_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $H(z)\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $z\rightarrow z_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Also,
 due to the L'Hopital's theorem,
 
\begin_inset Formula $H(z)\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $z\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 takes a globally U-shaped curve.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $H'(z)=\frac{W(z)}{S(z)^{2}}$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula $W(z)=T'(z)S(z)-T(z)S'(z)$
\end_inset

 and is summrized as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W(z)=\frac{2z(p(z)+q(z)u)}{(z^{2}+1)^{2}},
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $u=\sqrt{z^{2}+1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

$$
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{aligned}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

p(z)={}&3z^{16}+4z^{14}-41z^{12}+8z^{11}-167z^{10}+74z^{9}-285z^{8}+134z^{7}-274z^{6}
\backslash

