\end_inset

From Sturm's theorem,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda=0$
\end_inset

 has at most three solutions.
 Also,
 we can check that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda(0)<0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda(1/2)>0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda(3/2)>0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda(2)<0$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda(3)>0$
\end_inset

.
 Hence,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda=0$
\end_inset

 has three solutions 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{0},l_{1},l_{2}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $0<l_{0}<1/2$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $3/2<l_{1}<2<l_{2}<3$
\end_inset

 hold.
 Note that these solutions make 
\begin_inset Formula $B=0$
\end_inset

,
 while 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 must have the opposing sign when 
\begin_inset Formula $W=0$
\end_inset

.
 This is achieved only by 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{m}=l_{1}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $l_{0}<z_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{2}$
\end_inset

 makes sign
\begin_inset Formula $p=$
\end_inset

sign
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

).
 Hence,
 we conclude that 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 has a uniqe local minimum at 
\begin_inset Formula $z=z_{m}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In summary,
 we have the following result:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\sigma_{\eta}^{2}}{\sigma_{u}^{2}}\le H_{m}\equiv H(z_{m})$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is monotonically increasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $z>z_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 there is a unique solution to the fixed point problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\sigma_{\eta}^{2}}{\sigma_{u}^{2}}>H_{m}$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $H(z)=\kappa^{2}$
\end_inset

 has two solutions,
 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{h}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{l}(>z_{h})$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 takes its local maximum 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{h}=G(z_{h})$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{h}$
\end_inset

 and its local minimum 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{l}=G(z_{l})$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{l}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta<\text{\underbar{\ensuremath{\theta}}}\equiv G_{l}$
\end_inset

,
 then the low-
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 equilibrium is a unique solution;
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{\underbar{\ensuremath{\theta}}}<\theta<\bar{\theta}\equiv G_{h}$
\end_inset

,
 then the low- and high-
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 equilibria coexist;
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{\theta}<\theta$
\end_inset

,
 then the high-
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 equilibrium is a uniqe solution.
\end_layout

